{"event_schema": "1.0.0", "run_id": "ab1cfefc-4c43-4f04-945f-90965f33f82e", "seq": 53, "event_type": "model_response", "utc": "2026-09-28T18:45:41.224541Z", "monotonic_ms": 70312, "case_id": "case_002", "provider": "anthropic", "model_id": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "turn_index": 11, "duration_ms": 6109, "content": "{\"tool\": \"disposition\", \"args\": {\"category\": \"ICU\", \"urgency\": \"emergent\", \"rationale\": \"Symptomatic bradycardia (HR 42/min) with no P waves and a narrow-QRS junctional escape rhythm. He also has right-sided heart failure: edema, abdominal pain, dilated RV with impaired function, severe TR, and estimated PASP 58 mm Hg. hs-TnT is mildly elevated at 31 ng/L. Cardiac MRI shows left atrial late gadolinium enhancement with borderline T1/T2 edema. The likely cause is atrial myocarditis or atrial cardiomyopathy with atrial standstill. Other possible causes are sarcoidosis, amyloidosis, and infection. He needs continuous monitoring and readiness for temporary transvenous pacing. Atropine was ordered, but its response is not documented. Potassium, creatinine, TSH, and BNP results were not available.\", \"followup_or_handoff\": \"Admit to the ICU or CCU under cardiology and electrophysiology. Keep transcutaneous pacing pads on and continuous telemetry running. Prepare for temporary transvenous pacing, and consider a permanent pacemaker after the workup. Obtain the missing potassium, creatinine, TSH, BNP, and liver function results. Test for Lyme and other infections, and consider workup for sarcoidosis and amyloidosis, including PET-CT and endomyocardial biopsy if needed. Give cautious diuretics for volume overload. Avoid AV nodal blocking drugs. Review family history and genetic testing for atrial standstill.\"}}", "tool_transport": "json_emulated", "tool_calls_count": 1, "provider_metadata": {"transport": "claude_code_subscription_cli_json_action", "tool_transport": "json_emulated", "subtype": "success", "model_requested": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "models